## 2. Root cause: where the seconds actually go

### 2.1 The captured hot path

Three goroutines in the two dumps are in state `[runnable]` (i.e. burning CPU, not blocked). **All
three are in the identical stack**, one in `internal/install` (goroutine 1115) and two in
`atomicity` (goroutines 261, 290):

```
strings.FieldsFunc                          strings/strings.go:441
transaction.namespaceComponents             internal/transaction/namespace.go:221
transaction.namespaceContains               internal/transaction/namespace.go:205
transaction.namespacePathsOverlap           internal/transaction/namespace.go:178
transaction.validateIndependentTargetNamespaces  internal/transaction/namespace.go:104
transaction.validateJournal                 internal/transaction/journal.go:344   (install, atomicity/project)
transaction.(*Engine).validateJournal       internal/transaction/journal.go:354   (atomicity/global)
transaction.(*Engine).saveJournal           internal/transaction/journal.go:72
transaction.(*Engine).stageTarget / copyStagingFile   internal/transaction/staging.go:37 / :161
transaction.(*Engine).Prepare               internal/transaction/engine.go:78
install.runCommit                           internal/install/commit.go:661
```

**Honesty caveat:** three stack samples are not a profile. This is strong evidence — three
independent goroutines across two separate test binaries, all CPU-runnable in the same leaf — but
it is not a measured attribution. Nothing in the recommended patch depends on this being the
dominant cost; it is reported because it explains the shape and because it points at a much
cheaper production-side fix (§6).

### 2.2 Why that path is expensive (static reading of the source)

`Engine.saveJournal` (`journal.go:71`) calls `Engine.validateJournal` (`journal.go:350`), which runs
`validateIndependentTargetNamespaces` **twice** per save — once inside `validateJournal(journal)`
(`journal.go:344`) and once with the engine's reserved journal-root path (`journal.go:354`).

Each run of `validateIndependentTargetNamespaces` (`namespace.go:26`):

1. Rebuilds up to `len(targets)*7 + len(reserved)` canonical paths. Per path it calls
   `canonicalNamespaceTargetPath` (→ `filepath.EvalSymlinks`, one syscall per component),
   `namespaceCaseInsensitive` (`namespace_case_darwin.go:13` → existing-ancestor walk +
   `unix.Pathconf`) and `namespaceNormalizationInsensitive` (`namespace_case_darwin.go:25` →
   ancestor walk + `unix.Statfs`).
2. Then runs an **O(P²)** pairwise loop (`namespace.go:100-111`) over those paths. Each pair calls
   `namespacePathsOverlap` → `namespaceContains` in both directions, and each of those splits both
   paths into components (`namespaceComponents`, `namespace.go:217`). On APFS/HFS
   `normInsensitive` is **true**, so `namespaceComponentEqual` (`namespace.go:230`) NFD-normalizes
   every component of both sides of every comparison. Non-containing pairs additionally do
   `os.Stat`/`os.Lstat` via `namespaceIdentity`.

`saveJournal` is called **3× per staging entry** (`staging.go:26`, `:33`, `:56`) and **2× per
32 KiB copy chunk** (`staging.go:141`, `:161`), plus commit/rollback saves (`:218`, `:244`).

Target counts are visible in the dump frames: `validateIndependentTargetNamespaces({…, 0x8, …})` =
**8 targets** for `internal/install`, and `0x13` / `0x14` = **19 / 20 targets** for the two
atomicity scenarios. With `P ≈ 7·T`, the atomicity scenarios pay roughly `(7·20)²/2 ≈ 9,800`
pairwise comparisons — twice — for *every* journal save. That is a ~6× quadratic penalty of
atomicity over `internal/install` per save, on top of more saves.

### 2.3 The consequence that drives the patch

Because the fault is injected at `PointAfterBackup`, which is a **commit-phase** boundary, every
fault injection first runs a **complete `Prepare`** — all 19–20 targets staged, every entry
journalled — before the fault can fire. **Injection cost is therefore approximately independent of
which class is faulted.** This is directly visible in the alarm: the 4th project injection
(`50-env-file`) was 52s in and the 5th global injection (`80-removal`) 44s in, i.e. the same order
as any other injection, and the dump shows `Prepare`/`stageTarget` on the stack, not commit.

This is the single most load-bearing fact for the plan: the sweep's cost is
`(number of installs in the chain) × (per-install cost)`, and the only test-only lever on the first
factor is **partitioning the chain**, because the chain is inherently serial.

---

## 3. Derived per-install and per-package cost model

All derivations use only the quoted numbers above.

### `internal/install`

- 70 of 107 tests consumed 600s under race.
- Non-race share of those 70 tests, assuming uniform average cost:
  `(70/107) × 341.415s = 223.3s`. Implied **race factor = 600 / 223.3 = 2.69×**.
- Projected full race duration: `341.415 × 2.69 = 918s`, cross-checked by
  `(107/70) × 600 = 917s`. **Two independent derivations agree: ≈ 918s.**
- Overshoot vs the 600s alarm: **+53%**. To land at 480s (a 2-minute margin) the package needs a
  **≥48% wall-clock reduction**.
- Static inventory: **107 top-level tests, 109 install invocations, 84 `newEnv`, 60 `e.skill`**, and
  **zero `t.Parallel()` anywhere in the package**. The whole package is one serial thread on a
  16-core host.

### `internal/install/atomicity`

- Project chain = 1 baseline + 7 injections + 1 final success = **9 installs**. At the alarm it had
  completed baseline + 3 injections and was 52s into the 5th install: `508 / 4.45 ≈ **114s per
  project install** under race`.
- Global chain = 1 baseline + 5 injections + 1 final = **7 installs**. Completed baseline + 4
  injections, 44s into the 6th: `508 / 5.47 ≈ **93s per global install**`.
- Projected full sweep: project `9 × 114 = 1026s`, global `7 × 93 = 651s`; the sweep runs both in
  parallel, so sweep wall ≈ **1026s**.
- Projected package: `95s (pre-sweep) + 1026s ≈ **1121s**` — **+86%** over the alarm.
- Cross-check: non-race sweep ≈ `441.122 − 68 = 373s` (pre-sweep non-race estimated as `95 / 1.4`
  using the package's own light-test race factor). `1026 / 373 = 2.75×` — consistent with the
  2.69× derived independently for `internal/install`. **The two packages agree on the race factor.**
- To land at 480s the longest sweep chain must be **≤ 3 installs** (`3 × 114 = 342s`).

---

## 4. Recommended smallest test-only patch

Two changes, both structural, **no assertion removed, no case skipped, no timeout flag, no
production file touched**.

### Patch A — `internal/install`: mark 88 of 107 tests `t.Parallel()`

One line (`t.Parallel()`) as the first statement of each allowlisted test. Nothing else changes.

**Why it is safe here.** Every test builds its own fixture from three independent `t.TempDir()`
roots (`install_test.go:27-37`: `skillsRoot`, `home`, `project`), so there is no shared home, no
shared project, no shared manager lock, and no shared consumer ledger between tests. Static sweep
found **no** `os.Chdir`, `os.Setenv`, `os.Stdout`/`os.Stderr` capture, `GOMAXPROCS`,
`runtime.NumGoroutine`, fixed port, `time.Sleep`, `os.Getwd`, or `TestMain` anywhere in the
package's tests.

**Exclusions (19 tests must stay sequential).** Two hazard classes:

*Class 1 — `t.Setenv` (Go panics if called after `t.Parallel`; the variable is process-global):*

| Test | Anchor |
| --- | --- |
| `TestBuildRootExcludedBeforeLocaleRenderingWithoutCompilerExecution` | `install_test.go:169` |
| `TestRuntimeLauncherCapturesDeclaredSystemDependency` | `install_test.go:308` |
| `TestGlobalInstall` | `install_test.go:663` |
| `TestDefaultToolchainProbeRemovesItsProbeRootOnFailure` | `stage_test.go:1537` |
| `TestDefaultToolchainProbeRemovesItsProbeRootOnSuccess` | `stage_test.go:1554` |
| `TestDefaultToolchainEstablishRemovesItsPrivateRootOnFailure` | `stage_test.go:1570` |
| `TestProjectDryRunKeepsEveryEphemeralPathInOneOperationPrivateRoot` | `private_test.go:133` (via `isolateTempDir`, `private_test.go:20`) |
| `TestGlobalDryRunKeepsEveryEphemeralPathInOneOperationPrivateRoot` | `private_test.go:175` |
| `TestDryRunRemovesItsOperationPrivateRootOnFailure` | `private_test.go:222` |
| `TestGlobalDryRunRemovesItsOperationPrivateRootOnFailure` | `private_test.go:244` |
| `TestRealRunKeepsEveryEphemeralPathInOneOperationPrivateRoot` | `private_test.go:264` |

*Class 2 — the `afterDocumentOpen` package-level hook.* `internal/install/generation.go:57`
declares `var afterDocumentOpen func(path string)`, read unsynchronised on every document read
(`generation.go:81`). Two test helpers assign it: `duringDocumentRead` (`aba_test.go:37`, cleared at
`:47`) and `onceAfterOpen` (`generation_test.go:44`, cleared at `:52`). **Running any of these in
parallel would produce a genuine `DATA RACE` on a production global**, so they stay sequential:

| Test | Anchor |
| --- | --- |
| `TestProjectManifestABAAroundTheReadRestartsClosure` | `aba_test.go:87` |
| `TestGlobalManifestABAAroundTheReadRestartsClosure` | `aba_test.go:118` |
| `TestSubstitutionsABAAroundTheReadRestartsClosure` | `aba_test.go:147` |
| `TestHybridActivationABAAroundTheReadRestartsClosure` | `aba_test.go:182` |
