**Ordering guarantee this relies on.** Go's `testing` releases parallel top-level tests only after
every sequential top-level test in the same parent has finished. The 19 sequential tests therefore
never overlap the 88 parallel ones, so their `t.Setenv` windows and their `afterDocumentOpen`
assignments cannot leak into a parallel test. This is why Patch A does not need `TestMain`.

**Expected savings.** The 19 sequential tests account for ~14 of 109 install invocations ≈ 13% of
package cost ≈ 119s of the projected 918s. The remaining ~799s is spread over 88 independent tests
bounded by `-parallel` (default `GOMAXPROCS` = 16):

| effective concurrency | parallel part | + sequential 119s | total |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2× (pessimistic) | 400s | 119s | **519s** |
| 4× | 200s | 119s | **319s** |
| 8× (realistic in the tail) | 100s | 119s | **219s** |

Even the pessimistic 2× case clears the alarm. Realistic landing zone **219–319s, margin
280–380s**.

### Patch B — `internal/install/atomicity`: partition the sweep, parallelise the rest

**B1 — one class per scenario.** `commit_atomicity_test.go:41` (`projectSweepScenario`) and `:95`
(`globalSweepScenario`) already take the class list as scenario data (`sweepScenario.classes`,
`:26`), and the test body already iterates a scenario table at `:144-147`. Change the table so each
sweep class becomes its own scenario entry:

- `projectSweepScenario` → 7 entries, `classes` = one of `10-context`, `20-runtime`,
  `30-shim-canonical`, `50-env-file`, `60-adapter-ledger`, `80-removal`, `90-consumer`.
- `globalSweepScenario` → 5 entries, `classes` = one of `10-context`, `40-shim-forwarding`,
  `60-adapter-ledger`, `70-mirror-ledger`, `80-removal`.

Each entry keeps its own `env` (`:154`), its own baseline (`:159`), its own `before` snapshot
(`:160`), its own upgrade (`:161`), its own class loop (`:163`, now one iteration), and its own
post-sweep success + class-order assertions (`:200-215`). Chain length drops from
**9 → 3** (project) and **7 → 3** (global).

**Required companion change for the global scenarios.** `sweepScenario.sharedUserHome` (`:38`)
carries the explicit invariant *"two scenarios must never share it: their snapshots would
overlap."* The parent currently builds one `globalUserHome` (`:135`) and puts its `.local/bin` on
`PATH` (`:141`). With 5 global scenarios the parent must build **5 distinct user homes**, create
each `.local/bin`, and prepend **all five** to `PATH` in the single `t.Setenv` call at `:141`. Then
give each global scenario its own home (replace the `sharedUserHome bool` with a
`userHome string` assigned per entry). The invariant is strengthened, not weakened: no two
scenarios share a namespace.

**B2 — parallelise the seven non-sweep top-level tests.** Add `t.Parallel()` to:

| Test | Anchor | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TestStableHybridActivationCommitsWithoutRestarting` | `activation_test.go:104` | own `newEnv` |
| `TestHybridDeclarationRemovedBeforeHomeLockRestartsAndCommitsNoStaleContext` | `activation_test.go:127` | own `newEnv` |
| `TestHybridDeclarationRetargetedBeforeHomeLockRestartsAndCommitsNoStaleContext` | `activation_test.go:148` | own `newEnv` |
| `TestHybridDeclarationAddedBeforeHomeLockRestartsAndCommitsTheNewClosure` | `activation_test.go:169` | own `newEnv` |
| `TestAdapterMirrorLinksAreJournaledAndRestoredExactly` | `commit_atomicity_test.go:229` | parent only; subtests already `t.Parallel()` |
| `TestStaleAdapterEntryIsRemovedBeforeTheConsumerLedger` | `commit_atomicity_test.go:325` | parent only; subtests already `t.Parallel()` |
| `TestStaleAdapterRemovalRollsBackToTheExactPriorEntry` | `commit_atomicity_test.go:380` | own `newEnv` |

`TestFailureAtEveryTargetClassRestoresPriorStateInReverseOrder` **must stay sequential** — it calls
`t.Setenv("PATH", …)` at `:141`, which panics after `t.Parallel`.

**Expected savings.** Longest chain `3 × 114s = 342s`; the seven non-sweep tests collapse from ~95s
sequential to ~40s and run after the sweep. Package projection **≈ 382s (382–450s allowing for
contention), margin 150–218s** against the unchanged 600s alarm.

Total CPU rises from 16 to 36 installs (`7×3 + 5×3`), i.e. **+125%**. Floor check:
`(7×3×114 + 5×3×93) / 16 cores = 261s` — below the 342s chain length, so the chain, not the core
count, remains the binding constraint on a 16-core host.

### Optional lever, not counted in the margin

`atomicity/fixture_test.go:83` writes `references/info.md` into every fixture skill. Grep over the
whole `atomicity` package finds **exactly one occurrence** — the write itself; nothing asserts on
it. Dropping it removes staging entries per skill context, and `saveJournal` (hence the quadratic
namespace validation) is called 3× per staging entry. This is a genuine linear multiplier on the
dominant cost, but its size cannot be derived statically — the producer would have to count
`StagingEntries`. **Recommend measuring before adopting; do not rely on it for margin.**

